{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Lest there be among you, etc.</b> Therefore I need to make you swear — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 29:17), and he explained it well. And the reason is to say: I need to bring you into imprecation and oath now in this covenant out of my fear that perhaps the heart of some of you may be drawn to the abominations of Egypt, as you did with the calf, or to the abominations of Ammon and Moab which you saw on the way, as happened to you with Baal Peor, for in the first covenant at the time of the giving of Torah there were no imprecations and curses there. And he mentioned \"man or woman\" because the woman's mind is light upon her, and so he does likewise concerning idolatry (Deuteronomy 17:2). And he mentioned individuals and family and tribe — which is a numerous people — and did not say this of all of them, as a matter of honor, or because it is impossible that the heart of all of them would turn today toward idolatry, for they would not enter into the covenant of HaShem and into His imprecation — for who could compel them? But a family or a tribe would enter into the covenant out of fear of the majority. And he said, \"lest there be among you, etc., whose heart turns away today\" — concerning one already drawn to idolatry who believes in it in his heart this very day; or \"lest there be among you\" an evil root that will sprout and flourish, and in days to come will put forth evil blossoms and yield wormwood — this concerns one who is not here with us today, for the father is the root and the son is a shoot; \"from his roots he will bear fruit.\" And he mentioned the root to say that he can bring the coming generations into the imprecation, for the root from which they will sprout is before Him today, and he enters into this covenant and imprecation. And he said \"bearing rosh\" to hint that from a sweet root nothing bitter emerges, and anyone whose heart is whole with the honorable HaShem and never had any thought of idolatry at all will not beget one who acknowledges it. And do not press me on this from the verse (Ezekiel 18:10), \"and he begets a violent son,\" for the truth of the matter is a great secret that I cannot explain:" "<b>And rosh and wormwood</b> — bitter or deadly herbs, and so are their names in the sacred language; or they are epithets: one is called <b>רֹאשׁ</b> because it is the head of bitter herbs, more bitter than all of them, and the second is called <b>לַעֲנָה</b> from the root meaning \"to be afflicted\" (לֵעָנוֹת מִפָּנַי, Exodus 10:3), for one who eats it is pressed and afflicted — to afflict himself he would eat it:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The colon at the end of the Hebrew indicates Ramban deliberately breaks off at a kabbalistic/sod passage; translated literally without expansion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:42.230892837Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}